            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+manifold"} Riemannian manifold
            is a
            %em hyperkähler manifold
            if its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holonomy+group"} holonomy group
            is (a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subgroup"} subgroup
            of) the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quaternionic+unitary+group"} quaternionic unitary group
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sp%28n%29"} Sp(n)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/central+product+group"} central product group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sp%28n%29.Sp%281%29"} Sp(n).Sp(1)
            this means that hyper-Kähler manifolds are special cases of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quaternion-K%C3%A4hler+manifolds"} quaternion-Kähler manifolds
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+manifolds"} Riemannian manifolds
            with full
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sp%28n%29.Sp%281%29"} Sp(n).Sp(1)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holonomy"} holonomy
          %p
            Equivalently, a
            %em hyperkähler manifold
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+manifold"} Riemannian manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
